p('<b>A conditional WRRA computation and a precise limit on address-color coupling</b><br/>Wonsik Choi and Jeongin Choi<br/>Independent Research, Seoul, Republic of Korea<br/>20 October 2026 | v0.8.2<br/>janefather@gmail.com | ORCID 0009-0001-4263-9772')
h('Abstract')
p('We report a bounded reproducibility study of the particle/component branch of Minimal Computing Cosmology 3.0, distinguishing its arithmetic component outputs from its separately adopted color representation. Frozen-input replay reproduces 427,892 odd-composite addresses, phenotype weight 0.050000000000000065 and assembly-order total-variation distance 0.20472100116369085. The supplied antisymmetric three-color state is annihilated by all eight total generators. Exact singlet support in three fundamental factors forces address-color factorization, restricting what a color-only interface can retain. Source inspection and restricted-namespace execution establish that the original color routine reads no arithmetic state. Together these results locate the interface boundary: order-sensitive component information remains in the address/component register, while the exact three-factor color singlet supplies a consistent but address-independent color sector. The work is a bounded reproducibility and implementation note; it adds no new SU(3) representation theory, particle identification or observational validation.')
h('1. Scope and reproducibility question')
p('The question is whether a successful combined Stage-3 replay supports each proposed claim about its arithmetic component and color interfaces. Component statistics and color invariance are different outputs of the same package. The replay measures an order ambiguity relevant to the component decoder; dependency inspection tests whether the color output depends on that decoder; the kernel proof restricts what the declared color state can encode. Neither branch alone answers all three interface questions.')
p('The contribution is a source-specific reproducibility record and dependency map for researchers inspecting or extending this finite model. Its structural finding is the separation between order-sensitive arithmetic assembly and an address-independent color contract. The original decoder is already labeled conditional: this note documents the existing boundary and does not attribute an unsupported claim or a deployed decoder failure to that source.')
h('Assessment contract')
p('<b>Inputs:</b> frozen address calibration, inherited assembly/charge rules, adopted three-color representation.<br/><b>WRRA transformation:</b> weighted address assembly and color checks feed a dependency and information-retention audit.<br/><b>Outputs:</b> reproduced component statistics, color residuals, exact factorization and a source dependency map.<br/><b>Falsification:</b> failed replay, a violated generator/kernel identity, a counterexample to factorization under the stated support assumptions. An unexpected arithmetic-state read in the isolated color routine would invalidate the dependency conclusion. Observed physical adequacy requires an additional stated mapping to observations.')
page()
h('2. Frozen component replay and the supplied color branch')
p('WRRA abbreviates Worldline-Residue-Resource-Action. The frozen upstream commit is 9d9daf21154e46e55b9fd0fd461c7c070156ab94. Active addresses are n=2,...,1,015,000, with normalized measure w(n)=n^(-alpha)/sum_{m=2}^N m^(-alpha), alpha=1.8996877935161325. The reported phenotype weight is W_phi=sum_n w(n)e_phi(n). The admission threshold h=1.4476744689338703 is retained without refitting. On odd composites, e_phi=1-product_k[1-sigmoid(d_k-h)] for k=0,...,7, where d_k=sum_j cos[gamma_j(log n+0.1k)]/2 and sigmoid(x)=1/(1+exp(-x)). Other addresses have zero phenotype effect. Adopted gamma values are 14.134725141734695, 21.022039638771556, 25.01085758014569 and 30.424876125859512.')
eq('p(n)=w(n)e_phi(n) / sum_m w(m)e_phi(m)')
p('For each pool of the first K prime labels, initialize floor(n/sum(primes)) copies of every prime, then repeatedly cycle through the chosen ascending or descending prime order, taking at most one copy of each prime per traversal when the residue permits, until the residue is below two. Integer reconstruction n=sum_j count_j prime_j+residue is preserved. The candidate fraction is the p-weighted count of historically selected labels {5,47}, divided by the p-weighted total component count. Particle names, those labels and the 16-channel charge table are supplied. The exact rational neutral motifs are uud+electron, udd, an electron/conjugate pair and an individually neutral channel.')
table([['Branch','Candidate fraction / mean component count'],*[[k,f"{v['neutron_fraction_5_47']:.12f} / {v['mean_parts']:.10f}"] for k,v in a['profiles'].items()]])
p('Define a_j=sum_n p(n)count_j(n) and P_j=a_j/sum_l a_l, aligning prime labels between orders. Then TV=(1/2)sum_j |P_j(small)-P_j(large)|=0.20472100116369085 at K=15. The upstream Stage-3 source already computes this nonzero TV and tests TV>0.1; the present audit reproduces, rather than discovers, that order dependence. Fixed chosen orders remain legitimate algorithms; an order-independent decoder is a stronger claim. No observed neutron abundance is inferred from these conditional component fractions.')
eq('|S>=(|rgb>+|gbr>+|brg>-|rbg>-|grb>-|bgr>)/sqrt(6)\nQ_a=T_a tensor I tensor I + I tensor T_a tensor I + I tensor I tensor T_a')
p('Standard fundamental generators satisfy Tr(T_a T_b)=delta_ab/2. The original color_check() uses G_a=2T_a, hence Tr(G_a G_b)=2 delta_ab; annihilation and the kernel are unchanged. It constructs the three-dimensional tensor, signed permutations and eight matrices internally, and reads no address or assembly data.')
